chx:Document a doc:ReportSection ; rdfs:label "Bitcoin Security"@en ; skos:definition "This document renews chapter 13 of the 3rd edition of Mastering Bitcoin for SEN0401 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13/research> ;
    doc:hasSection chx:S_SecurityModel, chx:S_ProtectingKeys, chx:S_KeepingAccess, chx:S_SharingControl, chx:S_CustodyHistory, chx:S_Survivability .
chx:S_SecurityModel a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "The security model"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The security model" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 1 ;
    skos:definition "The first subject is the security model of the chapter: why a payment can be public, why the network may be open, which single block a node takes on trust, and how a user checks the software before running it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Each statement was run on a private chain of Bitcoin Core 31.1 or read from its source (Bitcoin Core, 2026). This subject covers Transactions that can be published; Transport encryption; The root of trust; Verifying the software." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#SecurityModel> .
chx:S_TransactionsPublishable a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Transactions that can be published"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Transactions that can be published" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 2 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SecurityModel ;
    skos:definition "This section is about what a signed payment reveals and what happens when it is changed or sent again (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). This subject covers A signed transaction hides the key; Tampering breaks the signature; SIGHASH_ALL is the default; A replay pays nothing." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#TransactionsPublishable> .
chx:S_SignedTransactionHidesKey a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "A signed transaction hides the key"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "A signed transaction hides the key" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 3 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TransactionsPublishable ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's first claim is that a Bitcoin payment can travel over an open channel because it authorizes one value to one recipient and reveals no secret, in contrast to a card number that lets anyone charge again (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). On the test chain a wallet whose key we knew signed a payment of 222 bytes, and the spending input held exactly two witness items, a signature of 71 bytes and the public key of 33 bytes; the 32 secret bytes and their WIF text were nowhere in the transaction, which is what Nakamoto's design of anonymous public keys intends (Nakamoto, 2008). This is also why a transaction can be published to every node: the private key never leaves the signing code, and only its mathematical shadow, the signature, is shared. It is like signing a cheque with a pen whose ink cannot be turned back into the pen: everybody can see the signature, and nobody can use it to write another cheque." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#SignedTransactionHidesKey> .
chx:S_TamperBreaksSignature a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Tampering breaks the signature"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Tampering breaks the signature" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 4 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TransactionsPublishable ;
    skos:definition "A signature is not a stamp on a blank page but a seal over the exact content, so changing the content breaks it (Lau and Wuille, 2016). We took the valid payment and swapped the recipient's output script for an attacker's script, and in a second try lowered the first output from 1.5 to 1 coin; the original was accepted by testmempoolaccept and both altered copies were refused with mempool-script-verify-flag-failed, because the signature no longer matched the data it had to cover (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A block with the swapped version was refused too, which is the same rule one level up. It is like a sealed envelope with a wax stamp: a thief can open it and copy the letter, but a new address written inside breaks the stamp, and the post office rejects it." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#TamperBreaksSignature> .
chx:S_SighashAllIsTheDefault a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "SIGHASH_ALL is the default"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "SIGHASH_ALL is the default" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 5 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TransactionsPublishable ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's promise that a payment cannot be forged depends on what the signature covers, and the default type SIGHASH_ALL covers every input and every output (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The signature of the test payment ended with the byte 01, the code of SIGHASH_ALL, and the interpreter of Bitcoin Core 31.1 shows that other types exist, NONE, SINGLE and the ANYONECANPAY flag, while BIP 143 states that the hash of all outputs is used only when the type is neither SINGLE nor NONE (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Lau and Wuille, 2016). A signer should therefore know which type a wallet or device uses: with NONE the outputs are left open for whoever builds the transaction. It is like signing a contract on every page versus signing one page that says only that you agree to something: the first protects the whole, the second protects only what the page says." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#SighashAllIsTheDefault> .
chx:S_ReplayPaysNothing a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "A replay pays nothing"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "A replay pays nothing" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 6 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TransactionsPublishable ;
    skos:definition "The book contrasts the credit card, from which anyone holding the number can pull funds again and again, with Bitcoin, where a payment cannot authorize additional payments (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). We tried the replay: after the payment was in a block, sendrawtransaction answered error code -27 with Transaction outputs already in utxo set, testmempoolaccept said txn-already-known, and the receiving wallet held 1.5 coins before and after another block was mined, so broadcasting an old payment moved nothing (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The reason is the unspent-output model: a transaction spends particular outputs once, and a node that sees the same spend again finds that its inputs are gone. It is like a train ticket that is punched at the gate: showing the same ticket a second time does not open the gate again." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#ReplayPaysNothing> .
chx:S_TransportEncryption a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Transport encryption"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Transport encryption" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 7 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SecurityModel ;
    skos:definition "This section is about the encrypted transport between nodes, how it differs from the book's statement and how it can be bypassed (Mehta et al., 2019). This subject covers The book says no encryption; The v2 session on both sides; Downgrade to v1." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#TransportEncryption> .
chx:S_BookSaysNoEncryption a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The book says no encryption"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The book says no encryption" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 8 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TransportEncryption ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that because security rests on independent verification, no encryption is required for Bitcoin traffic, and it adds that encryption can still be useful (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's chapter 10 is plainer about the state of the software at the time of writing: Bitcoin communicates entirely in the clear, as does the modern implementation of Bitcoin Core, with Tor as the only encrypted option (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). That changed by default: BIP 324 defines a version 2 transport, release 26.0 added it switched off, release 27.0 turned it on, and the help text of 31.1 reads -v2transport Support v2 transport (default: 1) (Mehta et al., 2019); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2023); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2024); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2026). The value of the chapter's sentence is therefore that encryption is not needed for the safety of the money, while the reason to encrypt is privacy and the cost of eavesdropping; it is like a postcard that cannot be altered but can be read, now sent in an envelope by default." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#BookSaysNoEncryption> .
chx:S_V2SessionOnBothSides a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The v2 session on both sides"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The v2 session on both sides" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 9 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TransportEncryption ;
    skos:definition "The version 2 transport is an encrypted channel between two nodes, built from a Diffie-Hellman exchange and an authenticated cipher, and it exports a session id that identifies the channel (Mehta et al., 2019). We started two private nodes of Bitcoin Core 31.1 on the loopback address and connected them: both reported the transport v2 in getpeerinfo and the same session id of 64 hexadecimal digits, which is the value that two operators could compare by telephone to detect a man in the middle (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Nothing was captured on the wire here, so what we know is what the two nodes say about themselves. It is like two people who whisper in a language they invented a minute ago and then each read out the same code word to prove that nobody else joined the conversation." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#V2SessionOnBothSides> .
chx:S_DowngradeToV1 a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Downgrade to v1"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Downgrade to v1" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 10 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TransportEncryption ;
    skos:definition "Encryption in Bitcoin's peer-to-peer network is opportunistic: a node speaks v2 if the other side does and falls back to v1 otherwise (Mehta et al., 2019). A third test node started with -v2transport=0 did not list P2P_V2 among its services and connected to the listening node as a v1 peer with an empty session id, so that node had one v2 peer and one v1 peer at the same moment (Bitcoin Core, 2026). BIP 324 says that an eavesdropper who wants to keep listening must become active, by a persistent man in the middle, by downgrading connections to v1 or by running nodes of its own, and that is exactly what the session id allows to be noticed. It is like a shop that talks to every customer in private if the customer asks, and in the open if not: the open conversation is still possible, but the quiet one is the default." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#DowngradeToV1> .
chx:S_RootOfTrust a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "The root of trust"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The root of trust" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 11 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SecurityModel ;
    skos:definition "This section is about the single block that a node trusts, the check that it applies to its own blocks and the second anchor of the software (Bitcoin Core, 2026). This subject covers The genesis hash is hard-coded; Generateblock refuses a bad signature; The assumevalid shortcut." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#RootOfTrust> .
chx:S_GenesisHashHardCoded a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The genesis hash is hard-coded"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The genesis hash is hard-coded" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 12 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_RootOfTrust ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's root of trust is the genesis block, the single block that a node does not verify but is given, from which the chain of trust runs up to the current block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In the source of Bitcoin Core 31.1 the mainnet genesis hash is typed in and asserted, and the genesis block in the explorer data has exactly that id at height 0; the regtest chain of our test nodes has a typed-in genesis too, with no previous block (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Mempool Space, 2026). Every other block is justified by the proof of work that links it to this one, the chain of hash-based proof-of-work that Nakamoto's paper describes as a record that cannot be changed without redoing the work (Nakamoto, 2008). It is like the zero-kilometre stone of a road network: all distances are measured from it, and it is not itself measured from anywhere." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#GenesisHashHardCoded> .
chx:S_GenerateblockRefusesBadSignature a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Generateblock refuses a bad signature"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Generateblock refuses a bad signature" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 13 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_RootOfTrust ;
    skos:definition "A node enforces the rules even on the blocks it makes itself, so it will not turn a forged payment into a block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The call generateblock with the address-swapped transaction answered error code -25 with TestBlockValidity failed: block-script-verify-flag-failed (Script evaluated without error but finished with a false/empty top stack element), while the same call with the correct transaction returned a block hash (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The signature check and the script rules are the consensus check that every full node repeats, which is the sense of the chapter's advice not to outsource validation. It is like a bank clerk who checks the signature on every cheque himself instead of taking the word of the person who handed it over." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#GenerateblockRefusesBadSignature> .
chx:S_AssumeValidShortcut a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The assumevalid shortcut"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The assumevalid shortcut" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 14 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_RootOfTrust ;
    skos:definition "The book says that the only thing to be explicitly trusted is a fully validated blockchain, and for nearly everything that is so, but Bitcoin Core 31.1 contains a second anchor that the chapter does not mention (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The default assumevalid value in chainparams.cpp is block 938,343, mined on 2026-02-25 by F2Pool, and the help text says the node assumes that block and its ancestors are valid and potentially skips their script verification, a saving that ends when the option is set to 0; on the day of the fetch the tip was 971,580, so the shortcut covered the chain up to 33,237 blocks below it (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2026); (Mempool Space, 2026). The help text speaks only of script verification, and the hash is part of the software that people download, which is why the release check of the next paragraphs matters. It is like a customs officer who has checked every parcel of a sealed container once and then lets the container's earlier contents pass on a stamp, looking only at what has been added since." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#AssumeValidShortcut> .
chx:S_VerifyingTheSoftware a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Verifying the software"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Verifying the software" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 15 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SecurityModel ;
    skos:definition "This section is about the check of the program before it is run: the hash, the signatures and the reproducible build (Bitcoin Core release signers, 2026). This subject covers The tarball hash matches; A signature needs keys." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#VerifyingTheSoftware> .
chx:S_TarballHashMatches a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The tarball hash matches"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The tarball hash matches" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 16 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_VerifyingTheSoftware ;
    skos:definition "The experiments of this chapter ran on a binary that was first checked against the published list of hashes, the smallest step of the advice not to trust what one has not verified (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The SHA-256 of the 90,293,352-byte tarball bitcoin-31.1-x86_64-linux-gnu.tar.gz, beginning b80d9c3e04da78fb, equals its line among the 28 lines of SHA256SUMS, and the same line is in the saved copy of the list (Bitcoin Core release signers, 2026). This catches a corrupted download or a mirror that serves another file, but it says nothing about who wrote the list. It is like comparing the weight printed on a parcel with the weight on the scale: it shows that nothing fell out on the way, not that the sender is honest." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#TarballHashMatches> .
chx:S_SignatureNeedsKeys a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "A signature needs keys"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "A signature needs keys" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 17 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_VerifyingTheSoftware ;
    skos:definition "A list of hashes is only as good as the channel it came by, so Bitcoin Core publishes SHA256SUMS together with detached signatures of its builders, and the user must check them against keys obtained elsewhere (Bitcoin Core release signers, 2026). The signature file holds 11 signatures; gpg with an empty keyring found all 11 of them and could call none good, exit code 2 and No public key eleven times, which is the honest answer when no builder's key has been imported and shows that the check cannot be done by the hash alone (Bitcoin Core release signers, 2026). The project goes further than signing: its Guix instructions say the aim is to reproduce the toolchain instead of blindly trusting binary downloads, so that many builders arrive at the same bytes (Bitcoin Core, 2026). It is like a notary's stamp: the stamp on the paper means nothing until you know whose stamp it is and have a reason to believe it." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#SignatureNeedsKeys> .
chx:S_ProtectingKeys a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Protecting keys"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Protecting keys" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 18 ;
    skos:definition "The second subject is how a key is protected where it lives: offline, behind a passphrase, inside a device, on paper or in a memory (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The watch-only wallet, the PSBT through a cold node and the encrypted wallet were run on test nodes, and the device, paper and brain cases were read from pages and computed (Bitcoin Core, 2026). This subject covers Cold storage; Wallet encryption; Signing devices; Paper and memory." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#ProtectingKeys> .
chx:S_ColdStorage a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Cold storage"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Cold storage" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 19 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ProtectingKeys ;
    skos:definition "This section is about keeping the keys on a machine that does not go online, and the PSBT that carries a payment to it and back (Chow, 2017). This subject covers A watch-only wallet cannot sign; A PSBT through a cold node." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#ColdStorage> .
chx:S_WatchOnlyCannotSign a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "A watch-only wallet cannot sign"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "A watch-only wallet cannot sign" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 20 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ColdStorage ;
    skos:definition "Cold storage in the book means keys generated and kept offline, and the practical question is how an online computer can then receive and prepare payments without ever holding the keys (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The answer is a watch-only wallet: our hot node imported only the public descriptors of the cold wallet, its wallet reported private keys disabled, walletprocesspsbt came back with complete false and no signature, and listdescriptors with private keys failed with -4 Can't get private descriptor string for watch-only wallets; the PSBT it built carried the key origin data that the signer needs (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2018). A thief who owns the hot machine can therefore see the balance and the addresses, which is a privacy loss, but cannot move a satoshi. It is like a bank clerk who may read the account book and prepare a payment slip but has no signing pen." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#WatchOnlyCannotSign> .
chx:S_PsbtThroughColdNode a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "A PSBT through a cold node"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "A PSBT through a cold node" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 21 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ColdStorage ;
    skos:definition "A partially signed bitcoin transaction is a container that travels between a machine that knows the coins and a machine that holds the keys, and BIP 174 names the roles of Creator, Updater, Signer and Finalizer (Chow, 2017). In the test the cold node had 0 blocks and 0 connections, took the unsigned PSBT of 528 characters, returned a signed one of 596 characters with the final witness, and the hot node finalized and broadcast it, giving 1 confirmation and 1.0 coin received; a second format, PSBT version 2, lets inputs and outputs be added after creation (Chow, 2021); (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The second node here stands in for an offline computer, but it ran on the same machine and the string passed through our script, so no true air gap or removable medium was involved, as the record states; and a real air gap is not absolute, since Wikipedia notes that viruses such as Stuxnet were designed to infect air-gapped systems through removable media (Wikipedia contributors, 2026). Wikipedia's article on cryptocurrency wallets describes cold storage as simply keeping the private keys out of reach of hackers (Wikipedia contributors, 2026). It is like a letter that is carried to a notary in another town: the notary needs the text and the seal, not the town's news." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#PsbtThroughColdNode> .
chx:S_WalletEncryption a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Wallet encryption"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Wallet encryption" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 22 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ProtectingKeys ;
    skos:definition "This section is about the passphrase of the wallet: its errors, its timeout, its parameters and its effect on the seed (Bitcoin Core, 2026). This subject covers A locked wallet refuses; The wrong passphrase error; The unlock timeout; The encryption parameters; Encryption makes a new seed; Stretching buys days." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#WalletEncryption> .
chx:S_LockedWalletRefuses a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "A locked wallet refuses"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "A locked wallet refuses" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 23 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_WalletEncryption ;
    skos:definition "Wallet encryption protects the keys on disk, and the simplest test is to lock a wallet and see what it refuses (Bitcoin Core, 2026). On the test node an encrypted and locked wallet still showed its balance of 1.0 coin, handed out addresses and listed its public descriptors, but sending and listing private descriptors both failed with error code -13, Please enter the wallet passphrase with walletpassphrase first, and unlocked_until read 0. The managing-wallets document warns that an unencrypted wallet is vulnerable if an attacker reaches the device where the wallet or its backups are stored, and that encryption may prevent that unauthorized access but also raises the risk of losing coins through a forgotten passphrase (Bitcoin Core, 2026). It is like a house with a front door that anybody may look at from the street and only the owner can open: the lock does not hide the house, it stops the entry." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#LockedWalletRefuses> .
chx:S_WrongPassphraseError a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The wrong passphrase error"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The wrong passphrase error" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 24 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_WalletEncryption ;
    skos:definition "The second half of the encryption story is the cost of a lost passphrase, and the node is blunt about it (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A wrong passphrase gave error code -14, The wallet passphrase entered was incorrect; after walletpassphrasechange the old passphrase gave -14 too and the new one worked, and the document of Bitcoin Core states that there is no way to recover a passphrase. This is the same lesson as the book's story of backups lost with their own keys: the more carefully the secret is protected, the more carefully its protection key must be kept, and the safest practice is to test the recovery before the money depends on it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). It is like a safe whose combination you changed and then forgot to write down anywhere: the safe is perfect, and so is your loss." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#WrongPassphraseError> .
chx:S_UnlockTimeout a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The unlock timeout"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The unlock timeout" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 25 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_WalletEncryption ;
    skos:definition "A wallet that must sign has to be unlocked, and walletpassphrase unlocks it for a limited time only (Bitcoin Core, 2026). In the test the unlock timeout was 3 seconds: unlocked_until was exactly 3 seconds ahead of the clock, a send inside that window worked, after 5 seconds unlocked_until was 0 and the send failed with -13 again, walletlock gave 0 at once, and the source caps the timeout at 100000000 seconds, about 3 years. A short timeout limits the time in which malware on the machine could use the open key, but it does not stop malware that waits until the user unlocks the wallet, so encryption is a layer and not a wall. It is like a door that you hold open with your foot while you carry the shopping in and which closes by itself behind you." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#UnlockTimeout> .
chx:S_CrypterParameters a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The encryption parameters"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The encryption parameters" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 26 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_WalletEncryption ;
    skos:definition "Encryption in Bitcoin Core is old, simple and documented in the code: the master key of the wallet is encrypted with AES-256-CBC under a key of 32 bytes with a salt of 8 bytes and an IV of 16, derived from the passphrase by SHA-512 rounds (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The default number of rounds is 25000, which the source says was just under 0.1 seconds on a 1.86 GHz Pentium M, 12.2 times the 2048 rounds of PBKDF2 in BIP 39, and RFC 8018 explains that a modest number of iterations, say 1000, is no burden to the legitimate user but raises the cost of exhaustive search (Moriarty et al., 2017); (Palatinus et al., 2013). On a modern graphics card 25000 rounds are cheap to try, so the strength of the encryption is the strength of the passphrase, and a long random passphrase matters more than any parameter. It is like a padlock of good steel on a shed with a cardboard key hook: the steel is not the weak part." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#CrypterParameters> .
chx:S_EncryptionMakesNewSeed a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Encryption makes a new seed"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Encryption makes a new seed" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 27 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_WalletEncryption ;
    skos:definition "The most surprising rule of wallet encryption in Bitcoin Core is that it replaces the seed: the RPC answered wallet encrypted; The keypool has been flushed and a new HD seed was generated, and the source and the managing-wallets document both say that a new backup must be made at once (Bitcoin Core, 2026). In the run the 32 bytes of the old key were found in the wallet file before encryption and not after, and the file grew from 24,576 to 36,864 bytes, which is a single-run observation about one SQLite file and not a guarantee that no trace remains elsewhere on the disk. Any coins received by the new seed cannot be recovered from the older backups, and the book's advice that a wallet can be backed up like any file is true only for as long as the backup is made after the last change. It is like changing the lock on your house and finding that the spare key you gave the neighbour yesterday no longer opens anything." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#EncryptionMakesNewSeed> .
chx:S_StretchingBuysDays a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Stretching buys days"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Stretching buys days" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 28 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_WalletEncryption ;
    skos:definition "Wallet encryption slows a guesser down by repeating a hash many times, and the repetition count is the only cost that a short passphrase can be given (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The header of the crypter says that the default of 25,000 rounds is just under 0.1 seconds on a 1.86 GHz Pentium M, so at that rate a passphrase of 30 bits, 2 to the power 30 candidates, costs a single machine about 1,242.8 days to exhaust and every added bit doubles it; the example changes the thousands of rounds and the bits of the passphrase. It is like a revolving door that takes a tenth of a second to turn: no single push is slow, but a billion pushes are. The rate belongs to that one machine, so another machine changes the days but not the doubling per bit." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#StretchingBuysDays> .
chx:S_SigningDevices a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Signing devices"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Signing devices" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 29 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ProtectingKeys ;
    skos:definition "This section is about devices that hold the keys: the signer interface of the node, the limit of what a device can protect and the PIN wait (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026). This subject covers External signer support; The device does not check the address; The Trezor PIN wait; PIN waits by attempts." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#SigningDevices> .
chx:S_ExternalSignerSupport a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "External signer support"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "External signer support" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 30 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SigningDevices ;
    skos:definition "The book expects that hardware signing devices may become the predominant way to store bitcoins, because they only hold keys and make signatures and have no general-purpose software to compromise (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Bitcoin Core supports this directly: it can be started with -signer=<cmd> so that an external tool, the example being HWI, signs for a hardware device, and the option is in the help of 31.1; the Bitcoin Wiki adds that the private keys are generated by the device and never leave it (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2026); (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026). BIP 388 explains why descriptors alone are not enough for such devices and defines wallet policies that the device registers, after which it can show the addresses on its own screen (Ingala, 2023). No device was available here, so the signer is read from documents and not run. It is like a bank's token that you carry: it knows how to sign but not how to browse, and that is the point." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#ExternalSignerSupport> .
chx:S_HardwareDoesNotCheckTheAddress a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The device does not check the address"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The device does not check the address" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 31 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SigningDevices ;
    skos:definition "A hardware wallet protects the key and not the intention, and the Bitcoin Wiki says so plainly: malware on a personal computer could monitor for high-value transactions and swap out the recipient's address, so the person must read the address on the device's own screen (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026). The same page lists Trezor first among the commercial devices in chronological order, which makes it the oldest of the list, and BIP 388 gives devices the means to show receive and change addresses on the secure screen (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026); (Ingala, 2023). In our tamper test the swapped transaction was refused because the old signature no longer matched; had the swap happened before signing, the signature would have been valid and the node would have accepted it, so the screen of the device is the last place where the human can still see the difference (Bitcoin Core, 2026). It is like a cashier who counts the cash carefully but trusts the customer's note about whom to pay: the counting is safe, the note is not." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#HardwareDoesNotCheckTheAddress> .
chx:S_TrezorPinWait a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The Trezor PIN wait"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The Trezor PIN wait" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 32 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SigningDevices ;
    skos:definition "A signing device must survive being stolen, and the Trezor does it with a PIN whose waits double after each wrong entry, 1 second after the first, 2 after the second, 4 after the third, and a wipe after 16 failed attempts (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026). Adding the waits before the sixteenth attempt gives 1 + 2 + 4 + ... + 16384 = 32,767 seconds, 9.1 hours, so an attacker with the device and no other weakness can make only 16 guesses in a bit more than nine hours and then loses the secret, and a recovery seed written elsewhere restores the funds on a new device (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026). The rule makes the short PIN adequate because the guesser cannot run faster than the device allows, which is the opposite of the file on a disk that can be copied and attacked offline. It is like a vending machine that locks for longer after every wrong code and empties itself after the sixteenth." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#TrezorPinWait> .
chx:S_PinWaitByAttempts a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "PIN waits by attempts"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "PIN waits by attempts" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 33 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SigningDevices ;
    skos:definition "A device that makes a thief wait longer after every wrong PIN turns a guessing game into a slow and finite one (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026). The wiki describes waits of 1 second after the first failed attempt, 2 after the second and 4 after the third, and a wipe of the device after 16 failed attempts, so the waits before the sixteenth attempt are 1 + 2 + 4 + ... + 2 to the power 14, which is 2 to the power 15 minus 1, that is 32,767 seconds or 9.1 hours; in the example the number of waits can be changed, and every extra wait doubles the total. It is like a bank door that stays shut twice as long after each wrong code and finally calls the guard: the thief runs out of patience long before the door runs out of codes. The doubling is a property of the described rule; how long the real device waits depends on its firmware." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#PinWaitByAttempts> .
chx:S_PaperAndMemory a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Paper and memory"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Paper and memory" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 34 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ProtectingKeys ;
    skos:definition "This section is about two old ways of keeping a key, on paper and in the head, and why both have lost favour (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026). This subject covers Paper wallets are obsolete; The brainwallet cracking time; Guessing time of a phrase." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#PaperAndMemory> .
chx:S_PaperWalletsAreObsolete a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Paper wallets are obsolete"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Paper wallets are obsolete" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 35 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_PaperAndMemory ;
    skos:definition "The book recommends keeping a seed on paper or etched on metal and calls it a paper backup, and that advice stands for a seed (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The Bitcoin Wiki now calls the paper wallet, a single private key and its address printed together, an obsolete and unsafe method that was popular between 2011 and 2016, one reason being that printers often have a hard drive that keeps the file and the private key with it (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026). The distinction is between a printed single key that has to be swept in full when spent and a written seed that feeds a hierarchical deterministic wallet, and the chapter treats both under the one word paper. No paper, printer or steel plate was used in this chapter; the claim is from the pages only. It is like keeping the only copy of a banknote's serial number in a drawer, instead of a book of cheques that can be revoked one by one." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#PaperWalletsAreObsolete> .
chx:S_BrainwalletCrackTime a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The brainwallet cracking time"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The brainwallet cracking time" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 36 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_PaperAndMemory ;
    skos:definition "A brainwallet derives keys from a phrase the owner remembers, and the Bitcoin Wiki says that common phrases, patterns, dates and substitutions can be tested with dictionary attacks and other offline guessing (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026). The arithmetic shows how small the space is: four words from a list of 2,048 give 2 to the power 44 possibilities, which a machine trying a trillion guesses per second, an assumed rate and not a measurement, exhausts in 17.6 seconds, while the 128 bits of a generated seed are 2 to the power 84 times more; BIP 38 answers the weakness of a human passphrase by using scrypt and salting to make each guess expensive (Caldwell and Voisine, 2012). The book itself asks users to use complex passwords, which is a hint at this danger, and a seed made by software is safer than a phrase made by a person. It is like a combination lock with four digits: the lock may be solid, but there are only ten thousand things to try." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#BrainwalletCrackTime> .
chx:S_BrainwalletGuessTime a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Guessing time of a phrase"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Guessing time of a phrase" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 37 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_PaperAndMemory ;
    skos:definition "A secret that a person invents and memorizes can be searched as a list, while a random seed cannot (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026). The wiki says that such a brainwallet can be tested with dictionary attacks; as arithmetic, a phrase of 4 words from a list of 2,048 words, the list of BIP 39 (Palatinus et al., 2013), has 2 to the power 44, 17,592,186,044,416, possibilities, and at a rate of 10 to the power 12 guesses a second, an assumed figure and not a measurement, they are exhausted in 17.6 seconds, and every added word multiplies the work by 2,048. It is like a padlock with four dials of 2,048 digits each: a patient machine tries every setting, and only truly random choices make the number of settings the real number of guesses. The example lets the reader change the number of words and the assumed rate." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#BrainwalletGuessTime> .
chx:S_KeepingAccess a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Keeping access"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Keeping access" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 38 ;
    skos:definition "The third subject is the opposite risk to theft, losing access: the seed words and the arithmetic behind them, the backup file, the stale backup and the descriptor that completes it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The word list and the key tree were recomputed against published test vectors (Palatinus et al., 2013). This subject covers Seed words; Wallet backups." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#KeepingAccess> .
chx:S_SeedWords a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Seed words"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Seed words" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 39 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_KeepingAccess ;
    skos:definition "This section is about the words, the seed and the master key that follow from them, tested against the published vectors (Palatinus et al., 2013). This subject covers The BIP 39 checksum; The seed from the words; The BIP 32 master vector; Core has no mnemonics; Words from entropy; The chance a typo is caught." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#SeedWords> .
chx:S_Bip39MnemonicChecksum a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The BIP 39 checksum"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The BIP 39 checksum" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 40 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SeedWords ;
    skos:definition "The book warns that the bigger risk is not theft but losing access, and the word list of BIP 39 is the everyday answer, because words are easier to write down and check than a long number (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A phrase of 12 words is 132 bits, 128 of entropy and 4 of checksum cut from the SHA-256 of the entropy; the 16 zero bytes of the first test vector give abandon eleven times and about, the library reproduced it exactly, the list has 2,048 words, and replacing the last word by zoo or abandon makes the phrase invalid, so most single typing slips are caught (Palatinus et al., 2013); (Trezor developers, 2026). The checksum can only tell that a phrase is wrong, never that it is the right one, and a word that is wrong but gives a valid phrase would open a different, empty wallet. It is like the check digit at the end of a bank card number: it catches the slip of a finger and does not know whose card it is." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#Bip39MnemonicChecksum> .
chx:S_Bip39SeedFromWords a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The seed from the words"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The seed from the words" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 41 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SeedWords ;
    skos:definition "From the words to the keys the path is a stretch and a tree: PBKDF2 turns the phrase into a seed, and BIP 32 turns the seed into a master key (Palatinus et al., 2013). The seed is HMAC-SHA512 with 2048 rounds over the words, with the salt mnemonic plus the optional passphrase; with the passphrase TREZOR the library reproduced the 64-byte seed and the master xprv of the Trezor test vector, and without the passphrase the seed begins 5eb00bbddcf06908, a completely different wallet (Trezor developers, 2026); (Moriarty et al., 2017). That is why a passphrase works as a hidden extra word: the phrase on the paper opens one wallet and the phrase plus the passphrase opens another, and nothing in the paper says which is the rich one, but the passphrase itself must then be backed up too. It is like a book code in which the page number is on the note and the book is in your head: lose either and the message is gone." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#Bip39SeedFromWords> .
chx:S_Bip32MasterVector a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The BIP 32 master vector"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The BIP 32 master vector" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 42 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SeedWords ;
    skos:definition "A hierarchical deterministic wallet needs one secret to make all keys, and BIP 32 fixes how: the master key is the HMAC-SHA512 of the seed under the text Bitcoin seed, with the left half as the private key and the right half as the chain code (Wuille, 2012). For the seed 000102030405060708090a0b0c0d0e0f the library gave a private key beginning e8f32e723decf405 and a chain code of 32 bytes, and the Base58Check form xprv9s21ZrQH143K3QTDL4LXw2F7HEK3wJUD2nW2nRk4stbPy6cq3jPPqjiChkVvvNKmPGJxWUtg6LnF5kejMRNNU3TGtRBeJgk33yuGBxrMPHi is the one printed in the specification, so the arithmetic is the same in every wallet that follows it. The practical meaning for security is that one backup of the seed covers all keys, past and future, which makes the seed the single most valuable and most dangerous piece of paper in the whole system (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). It is like the master key of a building: it opens every door, so it is kept in the safest drawer and copied with the greatest care." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#Bip32MasterVector> .
chx:S_CoreHasNoMnemonics a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Core has no mnemonics"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Core has no mnemonics" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 43 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SeedWords ;
    skos:definition "A reader may expect that every wallet backs up with twelve words, but Bitcoin Core does not: none of seven saved Core files, the sources of the backup, wallet and encryption RPCs, the documents on managing wallets, descriptors and PSBTs and the command line help, mentions a mnemonic or BIP 39 (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2026). Recovery in Core is by wallet file and descriptors, and since release 23.0 new wallets are descriptor wallets, while release 30.0 can no longer create or load the old Berkeley DB legacy wallets, which can only be migrated (Bitcoin Core developers, 2022); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2025). The book's chapter 5 does list BIP 39 and its successors such as SLIP39 among the recovery codes, so the two descriptions are compatible but not the same tool (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). This is a statement about the saved files only, not about every wallet program. It is like two keeps for the same castle: one written down as a list of words, the other as the architect's plan." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#CoreHasNoMnemonics> .
chx:S_WordsFromEntropy a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Words from entropy"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Words from entropy" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 44 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SeedWords ;
    skos:definition "The length of a seed phrase follows from a small rule and not from taste (Palatinus et al., 2013). BIP 39 takes the first entropy-length-divided-by-32 bits of the SHA-256 hash as the checksum, appends it, and cuts the sum into groups of 11 bits, one word each, so 16 bytes, 128 bits, with a 4-bit checksum give 132 bits and 12 words, while 32 bytes give an 8-bit checksum and 24 words; the example changes the number of bytes and shows the checksum bits and the words. It is like writing a number on a form with a last digit that checks the others: the longer the number, the longer the check. The BIP allows entropy of 128 to 256 bits, so a byte count outside 16 to 32 is not a valid phrase, even though the arithmetic still runs." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#WordsFromEntropy> .
chx:S_TypoCaught a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The chance a typo is caught"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The chance a typo is caught" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 45 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SeedWords ;
    skos:definition "A checksum catches most typing mistakes but not all of them, and its power grows with the phrase (Palatinus et al., 2013). BIP 39 itself says that the checksum is short and that, with its 8 bits for 24 words, 1 in 256 random errors will be missed; the same arithmetic for 12 words, with a 4-bit checksum, lets 1 in 16, 0.0625, of random wrong phrases pass, and the example changes the number of words. It is like the last digit of a card number: it stops most slips of a finger, yet a wrong number that happens to fit still gets through. A checksum that passes therefore shows only that the phrase is probably typed right, and never that the wallet behind it holds the coins the owner expects." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#TypoCaught> .
chx:S_WalletBackups a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Wallet backups"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Wallet backups" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 46 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_KeepingAccess ;
    skos:definition "This section is about the backup file, its age, its completeness and the book's story of a backup that could not be opened (Bitcoin Core, 2026). This subject covers Restore gives the same addresses; A stale backup misses funds; The descriptor is part of the backup; The lost seven thousand; What a backup range covers." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#WalletBackups> .
chx:S_RestoreGivesSameAddresses a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Restore gives the same addresses"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Restore gives the same addresses" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 47 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_WalletBackups ;
    skos:definition "A backup is useful if the restored wallet is the same wallet, and the node documents the whole round trip: backupwallet makes the file and restorewallet loads it under a new name (Bitcoin Core, 2026). In the test the source wallet and the restored copy both held 3.0 coins, the next new address of the restored wallet was the address the source handed out next, and the address far along the chain belonged to it, so a fresh backup restores addresses, coins and position in the sequence. The book says that a wallet can be backed up like any file and stored in several copies, and the point of the test is that the copy is only as good as it is recent (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Bitcoin.org advises regular backups at several secure locations (Bitcoin.org, 2026). It is like a photocopy of your address book: it is complete on the day it was made." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#RestoreGivesSameAddresses> .
chx:S_StaleBackupMissesFunds a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "A stale backup misses funds"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "A stale backup misses funds" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 48 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_WalletBackups ;
    skos:definition "A backup does not know what happens after it, and with a small keypool that can cost money (Bitcoin Core, 2026). On a node run with -keypool=3 the stale copy restored 1.0 coin of the 3.0, because the later 2.0 coins went to the tenth address, outside the stale range 0 to 2, while the up-to-date copy found everything; the help text of the option warns that smaller sizes may increase the risk of losing funds when restoring from an old backup, and the default size is 1000 (Bitcoin Core developers, 2026). A wallet that scans ahead 1000 addresses is rarely caught out, but an old backup and a busy wallet can still disagree, and after an encryption the backup is stale at once, as the earlier paragraph showed. It is like an old key ring for a building that has since added new rooms: the keys you have open the rooms that existed then." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#StaleBackupMissesFunds> .
chx:S_DescriptorIsPartOfBackup a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The descriptor is part of the backup"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The descriptor is part of the backup" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 49 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_WalletBackups ;
    skos:definition "The book's boxed warning says that to spend bitcoins you may need more than the seed, especially with multisignatures and complex scripts, because the output commits to conditions that the wallet must be able to reveal (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The node shows it: a wallet that held one of the three keys had no idea of the multisignature address (balance 0.0), a wallet given the full descriptor again found the 2.0 coins, and the same three keys in a different order under multi gave another address and balance 0.0 (Wuille and Chow, 2021); (Bitcoin Core, 2026). That is the reason for output script descriptors, for the descriptors of segwit and tapscript, and for the wallet policies that hardware devices register, and also the reason that a backup of keys without the descriptor is incomplete (Wuille and Chow, 2024); (Ingala, 2023). The record of the old wallets matters too: release 23.0 made descriptor wallets the default (Bitcoin Core developers, 2022). It is like having the keys to three padlocks but not knowing on which of a thousand boxes they hang." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#DescriptorIsPartOfBackup> .
chx:S_LostSevenThousand a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The lost seven thousand"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The lost seven thousand" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 50 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_WalletBackups ;
    skos:definition "The book's example of the opposite risk is a well-known Bitcoin awareness and education project that in July 2011 lost almost 7,000 bitcoin by losing the encryption keys of its own complex backups, and its moral is that securing coins too well may make them unfindable, like money buried in the desert (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Of the 77 saved sources only the book contains this sentence, so the event and the project are unconfirmed here, the name is not given by the book and we do not guess it, and the lesson, not the event, is what the table can support. The Bitcoin Wiki gives the same advice in the plain form that failed backups, mistyped letters and corrupted drives have lost many people their coins, and the only protection is redundancy (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026). Testing a restore before the money depends on it is the cheapest insurance, and the earlier tests of this chapter did exactly that. It is like a treasure map written in a code whose key was on the same map." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#LostSevenThousand> .
chx:S_BackupRangeCoverage a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "What a backup range covers"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "What a backup range covers" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 51 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_WalletBackups ;
    skos:definition "A backup knows only the addresses that existed when it was made, so coins sent beyond its range do not come back (Bitcoin Core developers, 2026). The help text of Bitcoin Core 31.1 warns that a smaller key pool may increase the risk of losing funds when restoring from an old backup and gives 1000 as default; on the test node with a key pool of 3 the stale backup covered the address numbers 0 to 2 and restored 1.0 of 3.0 coins, because 2.0 coins had gone to the tenth address, number 9 (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The example changes the pool size and the address numbers and counts what comes back. It is like a photocopy of a key ring: keys cut after the copy are missing from it. The numbers in the example are those of the recorded single run." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#BackupRangeCoverage> .
chx:S_SharingControl a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Sharing control"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Sharing control" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 52 ;
    skos:definition "The fourth subject is control shared among keys and among people: multisignature signing and key loss, the odds of loss and theft, the bytes a script costs, and secret sharing, which the chapter does not mention (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The signing was run, and the sharing scheme was computed (Shamir, 1979). This subject covers Multisignature; Sharing a secret." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#SharingControl> .
chx:S_Multisig a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Multisignature"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Multisignature" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 53 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SharingControl ;
    skos:definition "This section is about several keys for one payment: the signing, the order of keys, the loss of keys, the odds and the cost in bytes (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026). This subject covers Two-of-three signing; The order in sortedmulti; Key loss tolerance; The odds of loss and theft; Multisig costs bytes; The chance of being stuck; The chance of being robbed; The multisig size by hand." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#Multisig> .
chx:S_TwoOfThreeSigning a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Two-of-three signing"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Two-of-three signing" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 54 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Multisig ;
    skos:definition "The book recommends multisignature addresses for large amounts, with keys in different places and under different people, so that no one person can compromise the funds (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In the test a 2-of-3 wallet needed two signers: the first key alone left the PSBT incomplete with one signature, the first and third together made it complete, it was finalized and accepted by the mempool, and its input had four witness items, an empty dummy, two signatures and the script, in 146 virtual bytes (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Chow, 2017). The Bitcoin Wiki describes the same arrangement with keys spread across a desktop, laptop and smartphone, any two of which move the money (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026). Optech's separate page on scriptless multisignatures covers signatures made with several keys that look like one on the chain, which is a different technique from this script (Bitcoin Optech, 2026). It is like a bank vault with three keyholders and a rule that two must turn their keys together." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#TwoOfThreeSigning> .
chx:S_SortedmultiOrder a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The order in sortedmulti"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The order in sortedmulti" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 55 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Multisig ;
    skos:definition "Because a multisignature address depends on the set of keys, wallets must agree on their order, and BIP 67 solves it by sorting the public keys lexicographically by their binary form (Kerin et al., 2015). The three public keys in two orders gave the same address under sortedmulti and two different addresses under multi, the wallet's first address was the derived address 0, and the descriptor of the test was 436 characters long with three keys; the source of Bitcoin Core implements both functions (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A wallet that stores multi in the wrong order will not find its coins, which is the same error as the missing descriptor of the previous subject, and sortedmulti removes that source of loss. It is like lining up three people by height before taking a photograph: whichever way they arrive, the photo looks the same." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#SortedmultiOrder> .
chx:S_KeyLossTolerance a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Key loss tolerance"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Key loss tolerance" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 56 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Multisig ;
    skos:definition "The book notes that multisignature addresses can offer redundancy when a single person keeps several keys in different places (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). On the test chain, with one of the three keys lost, the other two still signed the PSBT to completion; with two lost, the remaining key left one signature and no complete transaction (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The Bitcoin Wiki says the same: in a 2-of-3 wallet the loss of one key does not lose the wallet, because the other two can recover the funds, and the redundancy of the backup is the point (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026). This holds only if the descriptor and at least two keys are kept, and a lost descriptor makes even three keys useless. It is like a car with three keys of which any two are needed to start it: losing one is an annoyance and losing two is a tow truck." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#KeyLossTolerance> .
chx:S_OddsOfLossAndTheft a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The odds of loss and theft"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The odds of loss and theft" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 57 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Multisig ;
    skos:definition "Whether a scheme of several keys is safer than a single key depends on whether one fears loss or theft, and the arithmetic of the binomial distribution shows the trade (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). If each key is lost with probability 0.1 and stolen with 0.05, independently, which is an assumption and not a measurement, one key is lost with 0.1 and stolen with 0.05; 2-of-3 is stuck with 0.028 and robbed with 0.00725; 1-of-3 is stuck with 0.001 but robbed with 0.14263; and at a loss chance of 0.5 the 2-of-3 stands where the single key does, at 0.5. So 2-of-3 improves both risks when each key is more reliable than a coin toss, whereas 1-of-3 trades theft for loss; real keys are not independent when they sit in the same drawer. It is like three alarm systems: with 'any one' you are never locked out but the burglar needs only one, with 'two of three' both the owner and the burglar have to work harder." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#OddsOfLossAndTheft> .
chx:S_MultisigCostsBytes a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Multisig costs bytes"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Multisig costs bytes" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 58 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Multisig ;
    skos:definition "Safety has a price in bytes, and bytes are paid for in fees. The same one-input one-output payment took 110 virtual bytes from a single key and 146 from the 2-of-3 script, 36 more, a ratio of 1.33, which at 10 satoshis per virtual byte is 360 satoshis more for each such spend (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The extra bytes are the second signature, the three public keys in the script and the empty dummy item, and BIP 387 defines the multi_a descriptor for tapscript, another script type with its own sizes that was not measured here (Wuille and Chow, 2024). For a safe that is spent once in several years the premium is trivial. It is like paying for a second lock on the door: a small recurring cost for a large drop in risk." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#MultisigCostsBytes> .
chx:S_StuckChance a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The chance of being stuck"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The chance of being stuck" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 59 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Multisig ;
    skos:definition "Several keys reduce the chance of losing access only if fewer than all of them are needed (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book says that multisignature addresses offer redundancy; as arithmetic, with 3 keys that are each lost with a chance of 10 percent, independently, an assumption and not a measurement, a 2-of-3 scheme is stuck when fewer than 2 keys survive, which has a chance of 0.028 against 0.1 for a single key, and the example changes the keys, the keys needed and the percentage. It is like a car with three keys of which any two are needed to start it: one lost key is a nuisance. Real keys are lost together when they sit in one drawer, so the independent chance is a lower bound and not a promise." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#StuckChance> .
chx:S_RobbedChance a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The chance of being robbed"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The chance of being robbed" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 60 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Multisig ;
    skos:definition "The same redundancy has a price on the side of theft, because a thief needs fewer keys than the owner has (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). With 3 keys that are each stolen with a chance of 5 percent, independently, an assumption, a 2-of-3 scheme is robbed when 2 or more keys are stolen, a chance of 0.00725 against 0.05 for a single key, while a 1-of-3 scheme would be robbed at 0.14263; the example changes the keys, the keys needed and the percentage, so that the reader can see how the threshold moves the danger between loss and theft. It is like a safe deposit box with three locks: more locks that must all open protect against theft and more locks of which one suffices protect against loss, but never both." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#RobbedChance> .
chx:S_MultisigVsizeFormula a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The multisig size by hand"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The multisig size by hand" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 61 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Multisig ;
    skos:definition "Safety is paid for in bytes, and the bytes can be counted field by field (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book defines four weight units as one virtual byte; a one-input one-output spend has 82 bytes outside the witness, 328 units, and the witness of a 2-of-3 script holds the 2 marker bytes, the item count, an empty item for the old CHECKMULTISIG, 2 signatures of 73 bytes with their length bytes, the script length and the 105-byte script, 584 units in all, which is 146 virtual bytes and 1,460 satoshis at 10 satoshis per virtual byte, the same figure that the regtest transaction measured (Bitcoin Core, 2026). It is like a parcel whose postage depends on its weight: every signature and every key is a heavier parcel. The formula holds while the script length takes one byte, which is true up to seven keys, and a signature one byte shorter lowers the weight by one unit." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#MultisigVsizeFormula> .
chx:S_SharingASecret a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Sharing a secret"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Sharing a secret" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 62 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SharingControl ;
    skos:definition "This section is about splitting a secret into shares, from Shamir's paper of 1979 to the formats of today (Shamir, 1979). This subject covers Shamir two-of-three; One share reveals nothing; Codex32 and SLIP39; Shamir by hand; One share fits every secret; Groups that open a secret." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#SharingASecret> .
chx:S_ShamirTwoOfThree a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Shamir two-of-three"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Shamir two-of-three" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 63 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SharingASecret ;
    skos:definition "Multisignature splits control among keys, and secret sharing splits a single secret into pieces, a distinction the chapter does not draw (Shamir, 1979). Adi Shamir's scheme hides the secret as the constant term of a random polynomial: with a degree-1 polynomial over the prime 2 to the 127 minus 1, the secret 123456789 and the slope 987654321, the shares were 1111111110, 2098765431 and 3086419752, any two of them recovered the secret by Lagrange interpolation, and one share did not; the paper calls this a (k, n) threshold scheme (Shamir, 1979); (Wikipedia contributors, 2026). The weak point is the moment of recovery, when the whole secret exists again in one place, whereas in multisignature the keys never meet. It is like cutting a banknote into three pieces so that any two taped together are enough to show the serial number." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#ShamirTwoOfThree> .
chx:S_OneShareRevealsNothing a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "One share reveals nothing"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "One share reveals nothing" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 64 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SharingASecret ;
    skos:definition "The strength of a threshold scheme is that fewer than k shares tell nothing at all, which Shamir expresses by saying that any k minus 1 pieces leave the secret completely undetermined (Shamir, 1979). Over the small prime 257 the share (1, 100) fits 257 pairs of secret and slope, one for each of the 257 possible secrets and 257 different slopes, so an attacker who steals a share cannot even say which secrets are more likely. Shamir also pointed out that with n = 2k - 1 the key is recovered even when k - 1 pieces are lost or betrayed, a very robust key management scheme (Shamir, 1979). The computation here is a demonstration over a small field and says nothing about the security of any product. It is like a jigsaw puzzle in which every piece alone shows a perfectly random picture." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#OneShareRevealsNothing> .
chx:S_Codex32AndSlip39 a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Codex32 and SLIP39"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Codex32 and SLIP39" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 65 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SharingASecret ;
    skos:definition "The chapter names multisignature and survivability but never secret sharing, and the word Shamir does not occur in it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The ecosystem has moved on: BIP 93, codex32, with the status Draft in the saved copy, splits a BIP 32 seed into up to 31 shares with a checksum that can be worked by hand, and the book's chapter 5 lists SLIP39 as a successor to BIP 39 that distributes one seed among several shares (Olsson Curr et al., 2023); (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The Wikipedia article on Shamir's secret sharing says that the scheme was first developed by Adi Shamir in 1979 (Wikipedia contributors, 2026). None of these was run on a device in this chapter. It is like a family recipe split between three cousins: each holds a part, and the dish can only be cooked when enough of them get together." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#Codex32AndSlip39> .
chx:S_SmallShamirByHand a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Shamir by hand"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Shamir by hand" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 66 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SharingASecret ;
    skos:definition "Secret sharing can be done by hand with a prime small enough to see (Shamir, 1979). The paper picks a prime bigger than both the secret and the number of holders and gives each holder the value of a random polynomial at that holder's number; with the prime 47, the secret 12 and the slope 30, the line 12 + 30x gives 42, 25 and 8 at x = 1, 2 and 3, and the first two shares recover the secret as 2 times 42 minus 25, which is 12 modulo 47, and the example lets the reader change the prime, the secret and the slope. It is like a banknote torn along a line so that any two pieces show the serial number. A prime as small as 47 is for teaching only: a real secret needs a prime far above it, as the 127-bit prime of the earlier example does." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#SmallShamirByHand> .
chx:S_OneShareFitsEverySecret a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "One share fits every secret"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "One share fits every secret" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 67 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SharingASecret ;
    skos:definition "The strength of the scheme is that a single share says nothing about the secret (Shamir, 1979). The paper states that knowing fewer than k pieces leaves the secret completely undetermined; counting shows why for a prime of 11, because the share at x = 1 with the value 5 fits the 11 pairs of secret and slope (s, 5 - s), one for each of the 11 possible secrets, so a thief holding it still has all 11 to try, and the example changes the prime and the value of the share. It is like a sealed envelope with a number that could belong to any of eleven locks. This holds for a polynomial chosen at random with a prime far above the secret; a careless choice of the slope would leak." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#OneShareFitsEverySecret> .
chx:S_ShareGroups a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Groups that open a secret"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Groups that open a secret" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 68 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SharingASecret ;
    skos:definition "A threshold says exactly which groups of holders can open the secret and which cannot (Shamir, 1979). In a scheme that needs 3 of 5 shares, any group of 3, 4 or 5 holders can, which is 10 + 5 + 1 = 16 groups, and the 10 groups of 2 holders learn nothing at all, because k minus 1 pieces leave the secret completely undetermined; the example changes the number of holders and the threshold. It is like a board of five directors who need three signatures: any three can act, and two together have no more power than one. The count only describes which groups may open the secret and does not protect against the moment when the secret is put together again in one place (Wikipedia contributors, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#ShareGroups> .
chx:S_CustodyHistory a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Custody history"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Custody history" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 69 ;
    skos:definition "The fifth subject is the history of custody: an exchange that held a country's worth of coins, another that called its wallets secured, and a coin with a sticker (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). These are read from the saved pages and their facts were checked against them (Wikipedia contributors, 2026). This subject covers Exchange failures; Physical coins." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#CustodyHistory> .
chx:S_ExchangeFailures a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Exchange failures"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Exchange failures" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 70 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_CustodyHistory ;
    skos:definition "This section is about custodians that lost customer coins and what the book draws from them (Wikipedia contributors, 2026). This subject covers The Mt. Gox loss; Bitfinex segregated wallets; The Bitfinex haircut; The Mt. Gox share of supply." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#ExchangeFailures> .
chx:S_MtGoxLoss a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The Mt. Gox loss"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The Mt. Gox loss" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 71 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ExchangeFailures ;
    skos:definition "The book says that many early exchanges kept all user funds in one hot wallet on one server and that many of them were hacked, and Mt. Gox is the largest case (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The exchange, based in Shibuya, Tokyo, handled over 70% of all bitcoin transactions by early 2014 and then closed after losing 744,408 bitcoins in a theft that went undetected for years, while the Bitcoin Wiki gives the loss as 640 thousand; the count of 850,000 fell to 650,000 when 199,999.99 bitcoins turned up in an old wallet, and in June 2011 fraudulent trades had already pushed its quoted price down to one cent (Wikipedia contributors, 2026); (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026). The Wikipedia article names its hot wallet, which fits the book's warning that centralizing the keys in a single system removes control from users and concentrates the risk. No server was attacked in this chapter, so the case is read from the pages and not run. It is like a bank that keeps the contents of every safe deposit box in one cash drawer by the front door, and then finds that the drawer has been emptied for years." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#MtGoxLoss> .
chx:S_BitfinexSegregatedWallets a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Bitfinex segregated wallets"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Bitfinex segregated wallets" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 72 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ExchangeFailures ;
    skos:definition "A custodian's design can fail even when it is sold as secured, and the Bitfinex exchange shows it (Wikipedia contributors, 2026). In 2015 Bitfinex partnered with BitGo to offer secured wallets, in May 2015 a hack took 1,500 bitcoins, and in August 2016 it announced a breach in which 119,756 bitcoin were stolen from the users' segregated wallets, after which the trading price fell by 20% (Wikipedia contributors, 2026). The page does not say how the keys were arranged, so we do not claim a technical cause; the lesson that is supported is the book's, that the customers had to trust the operators' security design, a trust that Bitcoin's own model does not ask for (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). It is like leaving your valuables with a guard who says the rooms are separate: the separation helps only if the guard's own keys are safe." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#BitfinexSegregatedWallets> .
chx:S_BitfinexHaircut a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The Bitfinex haircut"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The Bitfinex haircut" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 73 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ExchangeFailures ;
    skos:definition "A custodian that is hacked can share the loss among all its customers, including those whose accounts were untouched (Wikipedia contributors, 2026). After the August 2016 breach Bitfinex reduced every account balance by 36 percent and paid back in BFX tokens in proportion to the losses, so an account of 40 bitcoin kept 25.6 and had 14.4 turned into tokens, and the example changes the balance and the percentage. It is like a bank that, after a robbery, takes a fixed slice from every deposit and gives vouchers instead. The percentage is the one that the article reports; what the tokens were worth later is not part of this arithmetic." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#BitfinexHaircut> .
chx:S_MtGoxShare a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The Mt. Gox share of supply"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The Mt. Gox share of supply" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 74 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ExchangeFailures ;
    skos:definition "Numbers about a failure can be turned into a statement about the whole supply, and the statement shows how large the failure was (Wikipedia contributors, 2026). The article says that Mt. Gox reported the loss of almost 750,000 of its customers' bitcoins and around 100,000 of its own, around 7 percent of all bitcoins; 850,000 divided by 7 percent implies a supply of about 12.1 million coins at the time, and the example changes the percentage. It is like hearing that a vault lost a seventh of a nation's gold and working out how much gold the nation held. The figures differ by source, the Bitcoin Wiki speaks of 640 thousand (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026), so the implied supply is only as good as the quoted share." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#MtGoxShare> .
chx:S_PhysicalCoins a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Physical coins"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Physical coins" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 75 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_CustodyHistory ;
    skos:definition "This section is about bitcoin made physical, with a key under a hologram (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026). This subject covers The Casascius hologram." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#PhysicalCoins> .
chx:S_CasasciusHologram a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The Casascius hologram"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The Casascius hologram" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 76 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_PhysicalCoins ;
    skos:definition "Physical bitcoin makes the book's point about physical security tangible: Casascius coins were metal coins with a private key on paper hidden under a tamper-evident hologram (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026). Mike Caldwell of Sandy, Utah sold them until 26 November 2013 in 1, 10, 25, 100 and 1000 BTC sizes; reading the key without destroying the hologram was difficult or impossible, and FinCEN told him that minting physical bitcoins made him a money transmitter, which led him to suspend the sales (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026). The coins show the strengths and weaknesses of a bearer instrument: anyone who sees an intact hologram knows the key has not been read, and anyone who is given a coin has to trust the maker never to have copied the key. The book speaks of printed paper and metal plates; a coin with a hologram is a nicer package of the same idea (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). It is like a sealed envelope of cash with a sticker that tears when opened." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#CasasciusHologram> .
chx:S_Survivability a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Survivability"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Survivability" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 77 ;
    skos:definition "The sixth subject is availability after the key holder is gone: an heir path behind a timelock, the number that silently breaks it, and what this chapter did not run (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The script and its timelock were run on a test chain (Bitcoin Core, 2026). This subject covers Heirs; Limits of the experiments." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#Survivability> .
chx:S_Heirs a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Heirs"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Heirs" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 78 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Survivability ;
    skos:definition "This section is about a delayed path for an heir and the numbers that limit the delay (Friedenbach et al., 2015). This subject covers The heir waits ten blocks; The limit of older; The heir's wait in time; The sequence mask arithmetic." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#Heirs> .
chx:S_HeirWaitsTenBlocks a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The heir waits ten blocks"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The heir waits ten blocks" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 79 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Heirs ;
    skos:definition "The book says that the families of Bitcoin users are often unaware of the funds and suggests sharing access with a trusted relative or lawyer and, for a more complex scheme, multisignature access and estate planning (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A script can build a delayed path for an heir: with or(owner, and(heir, older 10)) the owner's spend was accepted at once, the heir's spend with sequence 10 was refused as non-BIP68-final at 1 and at 9 confirmations and accepted at 10, and the heir without the sequence set could not even finish signing (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The relative timelock comes from BIP 68 and BIP 112, and Optech's topics on timelocks and miniscript describe the tools that make such a policy readable and checkable (Friedenbach et al., 2015); (BtcDrak et al., 2015); (Bitcoin Optech, 2026). In real life the owner must move the coins before the delay ends to keep the heir waiting, and the lawyer, the court and the family were not part of this run. It is like a letter to be opened after ten days: if the owner turns up in time he collects it, and if he does not, the heir may." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#HeirWaitsTenBlocks> .
chx:S_OlderSixtyFiveThousand a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The limit of older"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The limit of older" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 80 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Heirs ;
    skos:definition "A timelock is only as good as the number it is written with, and BIP 68 reads only the low 16 bits of the sequence, so a wait is at most 65,535 blocks, about 455.1 days, or 65,535 units of 512 seconds, 33,553,920 seconds or 388.4 days (Friedenbach et al., 2015). The descriptor parser of Bitcoin Core 31.1 accepted both older(65535) and older(65536), but the heir whose key was behind older(65536) could spend with 1 confirmation, because 65536 has no bits in the low 16 and so means no wait at all, a trap that the parser does not warn about (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (BtcDrak et al., 2015). This is a single run, and it is the reason to test a recovery plan on a test chain before the real money is put behind it. For waits longer than a year the owner has to refresh the coins to restart the clock, which is a duty and not an accident. It is like a timer on an oven that counts in 16-bit seconds: set it to one more than the largest value and it silently starts from zero." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#OlderSixtyFiveThousand> .
chx:S_HeirWaitInTime a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The heir's wait in time"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The heir's wait in time" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 81 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Heirs ;
    skos:definition "A relative wait can be written in blocks or in time, and the two are close but not equal (Friedenbach et al., 2015). BIP 68 counts a block-based wait in blocks and a time-based wait in units of 512 seconds, chosen because blocks come every 600 seconds on average; a wait of 10 is therefore about 100 minutes in blocks at 10 minutes a block and 85 minutes in units, and the example changes the number. It is like giving an heir a letter to open after ten rings of a bell, where one bell rings at the pace of the miners and the other by the clock. The heir's script with older 10 behaved as designed on the test chain, and the block-based form depends on the real pace of mining, which varies." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#HeirWaitInTime> .
chx:S_SequenceMaskArithmetic a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The sequence mask arithmetic"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The sequence mask arithmetic" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 82 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Heirs ;
    skos:definition "A limit written in bits can silently turn a long wait into no wait (Friedenbach et al., 2015). BIP 68 says that a mask of 0x0000ffff must be applied to the sequence field, so the largest wait is 2 to the power 16 minus 1 = 65,535 blocks, about 455.1 days at 10 minutes a block, and 65,536 has none of its bits inside the mask and means a wait of 0; on the test chain the descriptor older(65536) was accepted and its heir could spend with one confirmation (Bitcoin Core, 2026). It is like an odometer that rolls over to zero after the last mark. The example changes the number of bits and shows the top value, the masked value of 65,536 and the days." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#SequenceMaskArithmetic> .
chx:S_Limits a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Limits of the experiments"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Limits of the experiments" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 83 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Survivability ;
    skos:definition "This section is about what the experiments did not do (Bitcoin Core, 2026). This subject covers What was not executed." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#Limits> .
chx:S_WhatWasNotExecuted a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "What was not executed"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "What was not executed" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 84 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Limits ;
    skos:definition "A security chapter must say what the experiments did not do, because a claim about a device that was never touched is a claim from a page and not from a run (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The record lists ten things that were not run, each with its reason: the hardware device, a true air gap, paper and metal backups, an exchange breach, estate planning with a lawyer, mainnet funds, Shamir in a wallet, BIP 39 recovery in Core, a good gpg verification and BIP 324 on the wire (Bitcoin Core, 2026). What was run is a set of private nodes on regtest, one record of one run, and every value read from it is a single-run value that a rerun will change in its hashes and times but not in its verdicts. The stories of Mt. Gox, Bitfinex and Casascius are read from the saved pages (Wikipedia contributors, 2026); (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026). It is like a crash test: the dummy and the wall are real, the family car's insurance claim is not." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#WhatWasNotExecuted> .
